{"source_slots":{"s0":"I’m looking for a specific note—ideally dated October or November ’97—saying the line was disconnected at the panel and capped, with a signature. Not just “fixed,” not “stopped sparking,” but clear language: “decommissioned” or “permanently disabled.” If it’s not written, then as far as I’m concerned, it was never officially killed. That’s the detail I need. If it’s just scribbled “repaired,” that could mean anything—maybe they just taped it over. I want proof someone with authority confirmed it was taken out of service. If there’s no mention at all, then it’s an undocumented ghost circuit, and I’ll push harder for the district to send someone with a tracer and a license.","s2":"I’m not just looking for a date—I need the exact phrasing that confirms intent. Words like “decommissioned,” “permanently disconnected,” “capped at source,” or “removed from service” with a signature. “Repaired” or “fixed” doesn’t count—that’s noise. Even “no longer in use” is too vague. I want the equivalent of a signed shutdown order. If it just says “wire disconnected,” I can’t tell if that was temporary or official. The custodian might’ve jotted it down casually. I need the language that matches district protocol—if such a thing even exists here. Honestly, half the entries from that year are in pencil. If I find it, I’ll know whether it was done right or just forgotten. Either way, I’ll bring you the exact wording tomorrow.","s4":"The line is officially called Circuit 7B—labeled that way on the master panel in the basement. But the logbook just says “east wing feed” half the time, so I’m not trusting titles. I’m looking for any entry where someone wrote “Circuit 7B formally decommissioned,” “permanently disconnected at junction box J4,” or “removed from service with approval.” Signature matters—ideally a supervisor or electrician with credentials. What I’m really trying to avoid is finding out ten years from now that someone just taped the wires together and called it fixed. If it wasn’t logged the right way, it’s still live in the books, even if it’s cold in the wall. That’s the risk.","s5":""Based on your detailed requirements for official, documented confirmation of decommissioning for **Circuit 7B**—the phrase you are seeking is the specific official language that confirms its official termination from service—I still require that specific language or documentation from your system administrator.**

**To proceed, please provide the exact technical term or the specific formal phrase used in the 1997 logbook that indicates its permanent 'decommissioning' status. Without knowing the specific nomenclature used in that specific logbook, I cannot provide you with the exact phrasing.**

**If you can provide the exact formal phrase or standard terminology used in your organization's maintenance logs, I can search the historical records for that specific 1997 entry."**"}}
